1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,192
The school Week Seven reading includes endocrine physiology, diabetes
medicines and endocrine emergencies. This workshop turns those topics
into an assessment and treatment-response sequence.

2
00:00:12,192 --> 00:00:23,904
Measure glucose early in altered function, seizures, stroke-like
symptoms and relevant acute illness. A known diabetes diagnosis is not
required to have a dangerous glucose problem.

3
00:00:23,904 --> 00:00:36,528
Correct confirmed low glucose promptly through the safe route while
supporting ABCs. A glucose result must not delay treatment of
inadequate ventilation or another visible life threat.

4
00:00:36,528 --> 00:00:47,712
Then ask why it happened and whether the patient truly recovered.
Persistent abnormalities can reflect recurrence, a second disease or a
different primary diagnosis.

5
00:00:47,712 --> 00:01:01,440
Endocrine cells release hormones into surrounding fluid and the
circulation. Exocrine glands use ducts for substances such as
digestive enzymes or sweat; their role is not simply waste
elimination.

6
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:12,672
A circulating hormone affects cells with the relevant receptors and
response machinery. More hormone is not always equivalent to a normal
response if the target is resistant.

7
00:01:12,672 --> 00:01:25,248
Receptor stimulation can change membrane signaling, enzyme activity or
gene expression. Catecholamine effects can be rapid, while many
steroid-mediated effects develop more slowly.

8
00:01:25,248 --> 00:01:36,912
Endocrine emergencies can result from deficient secretion, excess
secretion, resistance or disrupted feedback. Relate the mechanism to
the actual physiological problem.

9
00:01:36,912 --> 00:01:47,640
Negative feedback means that the response tends to reduce the original
disturbance. For example, rising glucose stimulates insulin-related
effects that lower glucose.

10
00:01:47,640 --> 00:02:00,312
Thyroid signaling uses an axis: hypothalamic releasing hormone,
pituitary TSH and thyroid hormone. Thyroid hormone feeds back to
reduce excessive upstream drive.

11
00:02:00,312 --> 00:02:11,064
The level of failure matters. A peripheral gland problem and a
pituitary control problem can produce different laboratory patterns
despite similar target-hormone deficiency.

12
00:02:11,064 --> 00:02:22,032
The field task is usually recognition and support rather than a full
hormone-level diagnosis. Ask about established disease, replacement
treatment and current stressors.

13
00:02:22,032 --> 00:02:34,416
The anterior pituitary produces hormones including TSH, ACTH, growth
hormone, prolactin, FSH and LH. Not all six are gonadotropins.

14
00:02:34,416 --> 00:02:45,096
The posterior pituitary stores and releases ADH and oxytocin made by
hypothalamic neurons. Production and release locations are therefore
different.

15
00:02:45,096 --> 00:02:57,000
ACTH stimulates the adrenal cortex to support cortisol production. The
medulla releases catecholamines through sympathetic neural
stimulation, not the same ACTH route.

16
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:07,368
These distinctions explain why control failure, replacement medicines
and stress can alter glucose, vascular responses and water balance in
different ways.

17
00:03:07,368 --> 00:03:20,400
Insulin supports glucose use and storage and restrains hepatic glucose
production, fat breakdown and ketone generation. It has ongoing basal
roles, not simply an on-off switch after meals.

18
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:31,416
Not every cell needs insulin for glucose entry. The brain and red
cells use largely insulin-independent entry, so the book all-cells-
need-insulin statement is too broad.

19
00:03:31,416 --> 00:03:44,016
Glucagon helps the liver make glucose available, while stress hormones
can raise production and reduce effective insulin action. Infection or
another acute illness can therefore destabilize control.

20
00:03:44,016 --> 00:03:54,960
The brain still needs a reliable energy supply, so severe low glucose
is urgent. Avoid replacing this principle with an exact universal
time-to-injury countdown.

21
00:03:54,960 --> 00:04:08,424
Diabetes mellitus is a group of disorders of glucose regulation, not
one uniform illness. Insulin deficiency, resistance and progressive
secretion problems contribute in different combinations.

22
00:04:08,424 --> 00:04:19,344
Long-term disease can injure vessels, nerves, retina and kidneys.
Neuropathy and cardiovascular disease can alter how an acute illness
presents.

23
00:04:19,344 --> 00:04:30,552
Ask about the actual treatment, usual glucose pattern, recent illness
and access to food or medicines. An insulin user is not automatically
a type-one patient.

24
00:04:30,552 --> 00:04:42,360
Diabetes can coexist with stroke, sepsis, trauma or poisoning. The
diagnosis should trigger useful checks rather than explain every new
symptom by itself.

25
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:50,784
Type one commonly involves autoimmune beta-cell loss and essential
insulin replacement. It can begin in adults as well as children.

26
00:04:50,784 --> 00:04:59,880
Type two involves resistance and variable impairment of secretion.
Some patients use insulin, and type-two patients can develop DKA.

27
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:11,136
Gestational diabetes relates to pregnancy physiology and needs the
actual pregnancy-specific plan. A general adult rule is not
automatically an obstetric instruction.

28
00:05:11,136 --> 00:05:22,944
Do not diagnose type from age, body size or a drug name alone. DKA and
HHS have typical associations, but overlaps and first presentations
are possible.

29
00:05:22,944 --> 00:05:33,720
Low glucose can follow excess insulin effect, insulin-secretagogue
medicines, reduced intake, activity, alcohol, renal disease or acute
illness.

30
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:48,072
Autonomic warning symptoms may include sweating, tremor, hunger and
palpitations. Brain dysfunction can cause confusion, poor
coordination, altered speech, seizures or reduced responsiveness.

31
00:05:48,072 --> 00:05:57,696
Some people have reduced warning awareness or a modified pulse
response. Absence of sweating or tachycardia does not exclude low
glucose.

32
00:05:57,696 --> 00:06:08,208
The locally checked treatment gate is below four millimoles per liter.
Interpret a reliable measurement with symptoms and treat promptly
through an airway-safe route.

33
00:06:08,208 --> 00:06:20,304
Low glucose can look like stroke, intoxication or a behavioural
crisis. Check it in a relevant altered presentation rather than
relying on appearance or a substance history.

34
00:06:20,304 --> 00:06:31,272
There is no age-fifty-five minimum or requirement for a known diabetes
history. A young or previously undiagnosed patient can have a severe
glucose emergency.

35
00:06:31,272 --> 00:06:44,472
After correction, repeat speech, limb function, face, awareness and
the full primary assessment as indicated. Preserve last-known-well
information when a stroke remains possible.

36
00:06:44,472 --> 00:06:54,864
A better glucose or partial improvement does not exclude a second
condition. Do not withhold urgent stroke evaluation merely because the
first number was low.

37
00:06:54,864 --> 00:07:06,408
Use the actual approved meter and sampling process, recording the
value in millimoles per liter with time. A device reading HI or LO is
not an exact number you should invent.

38
00:07:06,408 --> 00:07:18,576
Poor perfusion, sample contamination or technical error can affect a
capillary measurement. Follow the device checks and repeat or obtain
an appropriate alternative when the result does not fit.

39
00:07:18,576 --> 00:07:31,680
A continuous glucose sensor measures interstitial information and may
not exactly match rapidly changing blood glucose. Assess the patient
and use the actual confirmation process when needed.

40
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:46,248
If testing is unavailable or uncertainty persists in a critical
patient, obtain clinical support while providing the safe indicated
care. Do not give every unexplained coma an automatic glucose-naloxone
cocktail.

41
00:07:46,248 --> 00:07:55,824
For an appropriate conscious patient who follows commands and can
swallow without aspiration or choking risk, oral glucose is the
preferred simple route.

42
00:07:55,824 --> 00:08:06,696
The checked local monograph gives fifteen grams of glucose, or a
labelled tablet equivalent. The amount of glucose is different from
the total mass of a tube or pouch.

43
00:08:06,696 --> 00:08:18,600
The local repeat line is five minutes as required, with ongoing
assessment and repeat glucose. Do not put gel, a drink or loose gauze
into an unprotected airway.

44
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:31,392
Once recovery and safe intake allow, longer-acting carbohydrate can
sustain correction through the actual plan. Food does not replace
investigation of recurrent or unexplained hypoglycemia.

45
00:08:31,392 --> 00:08:43,752
For indicated adult PCP intravenous correction, the currently checked
pathway uses ten to twenty-five grams of dextrose, equivalent to one
hundred to two hundred fifty milliliters of D ten.

46
00:08:43,752 --> 00:08:56,232
Verify the bag and prescribed amount, assess a patent line and monitor
it throughout delivery. A previous flashback alone is not a permanent
guarantee that the infusion remains intravascular.

47
00:08:56,232 --> 00:09:08,424
Dextrose extravasation can injure tissue. Stop delivery and follow the
actual extravasation process with clinical help if pain, swelling,
leakage or another concern appears.

48
00:09:08,424 --> 00:09:22,344
The older Nancy D fifty recipe is not silently substituted for current
D ten. The monograph pediatric PCP line specifically says older than
twelve; do not scale the adult routine to younger children.

49
00:09:22,344 --> 00:09:32,376
D ten contains ten grams per hundred milliliters, or point one gram
per milliliter. Ten grams therefore corresponds to one hundred
milliliters.

50
00:09:32,376 --> 00:09:42,264
Twenty-five grams corresponds to two hundred fifty milliliters of that
verified concentration. These volumes change if the product
concentration changes.

51
00:09:42,264 --> 00:09:56,424
For comparison, D fifty contains point five grams per milliliter, so
its volume cannot be copied onto a D ten bag. Confusing grams,
milligrams and milliliters creates a large error.

52
00:09:56,424 --> 00:10:09,432
Read back the drug, prescribed grams, concentration, calculated volume
and route, then reassess. A correct calculation does not replace the
indication or a reliable line.

53
00:10:09,432 --> 00:10:21,120
The current local monograph uses a sealed three-milligram intranasal
kit when confirmed low glucose cannot be safely treated orally and IV
access is unavailable.

54
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:33,360
The source excludes intranasal use under four years. A second dose at
fifteen minutes if needed requires prior CliniCall consultation, with
a maximum of six milligrams.

55
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:46,392
This is distinct from the older one-milligram IM textbook or exam
reference. Verify the actual kit and instructions; the patient does
not need to inhale deliberately for nasal administration.

56
00:10:46,392 --> 00:10:59,640
Glucagon depends on available liver glycogen and can cause vomiting.
Continue airway support, glucose reassessment and escalation while it
acts; do not wait passively for fifteen minutes.

57
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:09,672
Recheck glucose after treatment and compare the actual function. A
better number without recovery needs another explanation and continued
support.

58
00:11:09,672 --> 00:11:23,784
Ask about insulin and oral medicines, intake, activity, alcohol,
kidney function and acute illness. A low-glucose episode can reveal
infection or a medicine problem rather than merely a missed snack.

59
00:11:23,784 --> 00:11:35,040
Insulin or secretagogue action can outlast the initial rescue glucose,
so recurrence is possible. Renal impairment and poor intake can
increase the difficulty.

60
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:47,304
Document timing, route, amount and response, then arrange the
appropriate care plan. One normal recheck does not automatically
justify leaving a high-risk patient.

61
00:11:47,304 --> 00:12:00,048
The local adult nonconveyance criteria require an explained fully
resolved episode, normal mentation and observations, glucose at least
four and no fever or concurrent acute illness.

62
00:12:00,048 --> 00:12:13,872
The page excludes overdose, excessive alcohol, oral hypoglycemic
medicines and another treated hypoglycemic episode within twenty-four
hours. Do not replace that list with personal reassurance.

63
00:12:13,872 --> 00:12:25,248
It also requires a responsible adult for at least four hours and the
follow-up and documentation process. These conditions are necessary
checks, not a universal discharge order.

64
00:12:25,248 --> 00:12:39,000
Use the actual capacity, informed-refusal and consultation pathway,
especially when any criterion is uncertain. A signature or a glucose
of four alone cannot establish a safe decision.

65
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:49,488
A forty-two-year-old using insulin has been unusually active and eaten
little. Now there is sweating, confusion and slurred speech, with
glucose two point four.

66
00:12:49,488 --> 00:13:02,040
Support current ABCs while a partner establishes last known well and
checks focal findings. The likely low-glucose mechanism does not
remove the need to assess stroke and other causes.

67
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:13,128
Determine whether the patient can follow commands and swallow safely.
Use oral treatment only if those conditions hold; otherwise use the
actual authorized nonoral pathway.

68
00:13:13,128 --> 00:13:22,704
Recheck glucose, speech, face, limbs, awareness and breathing after
treatment. Keep the timeline and all residual findings for the
receiving plan.

69
00:13:22,704 --> 00:13:34,752
In a separate original case, a fifty-three-year-old taking gliclazide
has eaten little during kidney illness. Initial treatment raises
glucose to six point five and improves function.

70
00:13:34,752 --> 00:13:46,296
Later glucose falls to three point one and confusion recurs. The
medicine and illness may outlast the initial rescue, so the first
improvement was not the end of the problem.

71
00:13:46,296 --> 00:13:58,320
Repeat the appropriate airway-safe treatment and assessment, seek
clinical support and arrange hospital evaluation. Do not treat this as
an uncomplicated resolved adult episode.

72
00:13:58,320 --> 00:14:10,392
Persistent focal findings after glucose correction also require urgent
stroke evaluation rather than repeated treatment for a now-normal
number. Match the action to the actual ongoing threat.

73
00:14:10,392 --> 00:14:22,104
When filtered glucose exceeds the kidney reabsorptive capacity,
glucose remains in tubular fluid. Water follows the osmotic effect and
urine losses can increase.

74
00:14:22,104 --> 00:14:32,976
The patient can lose water and electrolytes despite drinking because
thirst may not keep up. Vomiting, illness or reduced access to fluids
can compound the deficit.

75
00:14:32,976 --> 00:14:44,568
Hypertonic extracellular fluid also draws water from cells. This helps
explain both whole-body depletion and neurological dysfunction in
severe hyperglycemic states.

76
00:14:44,568 --> 00:14:55,464
Early frequent urination can become reduced output as perfusion or
kidney function worsens. Do not require ongoing polyuria to recognize
a late sick patient.

77
00:14:55,464 --> 00:15:07,320
In DKA, insufficient effective insulin and counter-regulatory hormones
promote hepatic glucose production and fat breakdown. Ketone
production can exceed safe handling.

78
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:22,464
Ketone-associated acids contribute to metabolic acidosis, while
glucose-related diuresis and vomiting cause fluid and electrolyte
loss. These processes interact but are not measured completely by one
glucose.

79
00:15:22,464 --> 00:15:35,352
Possible findings include thirst, vomiting, abdominal pain,
dehydration, deep breathing and altered awareness. Neither fruity
breath nor every classic symptom is required.

80
00:15:35,352 --> 00:15:47,832
DKA can occur with type two or a first diabetes presentation and can
coexist with infection, stroke or MI. Ordinary hyperglycemia does not
inevitably progress to DKA.

81
00:15:47,832 --> 00:15:59,280
Kussmaul-type deep breathing can be a respiratory response to
metabolic acidosis. Greater effective ventilation removes carbon
dioxide and helps defend pH.

82
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:09,312
Do not label this harmless anxiety or ask the patient to rebreathe
into a paper bag. That could increase carbon dioxide and worsen the
acid-base problem.

83
00:16:09,312 --> 00:16:24,168
Fatigue, declining awareness or airway failure still requires support.
If advanced airway intervention becomes necessary, the team must plan
to preserve needed ventilation rather than automatically impose a low
fixed rate.

84
00:16:24,168 --> 00:16:39,720
At PCP level, identify deterioration, provide effective basic
assistance and obtain appropriate advanced help. Deep breathing does
not by itself authorize intubation or an arbitrary very rapid bagging
rate.

85
00:16:39,720 --> 00:16:51,144
Osmotic urine loss and vomiting can deplete total-body potassium. At
the same time, altered distribution can leave the measured blood
potassium normal or elevated.

86
00:16:51,144 --> 00:16:59,160
These are different compartments. A high blood concentration therefore
does not prove that the body has an adequate total store.

87
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:10,296
Insulin therapy can shift potassium into cells and lower the blood
value. Definitive therapy needs appropriate electrolyte measurements,
monitoring and adjustment.

88
00:17:10,296 --> 00:17:22,008
Monitor relevant rhythm changes and request help for instability. Do
not give insulin, potassium or bicarbonate from a textbook algorithm
without the actual advanced authority and plan.

89
00:17:22,008 --> 00:17:35,448
HHS often develops in type-two diabetes with another illness and
inadequate fluid intake. Severe hyperglycemia and osmotic losses can
produce major depletion and hyperosmolality.

90
00:17:35,448 --> 00:17:47,712
Some effective insulin may limit extensive ketogenesis while failing
to control glucose adequately. Significant DKA-like acidosis is less
typical, but mixed states occur.

91
00:17:47,712 --> 00:17:58,632
Neurological findings can include confusion, focal-like deficits,
seizures or coma. The older name nonketotic coma does not mean coma is
mandatory.

92
00:17:58,632 --> 00:18:11,544
Assess infection, stroke, cardiac disease and other triggers as well
as glucose. Do not let an HHS label dismiss a new focal deficit or
postpone urgent definitive evaluation.

93
00:18:11,544 --> 00:18:24,192
DKA and HHS share hyperglycemic stress, fluid loss and electrolyte
risk. The relative ketone and osmotic components differ, and a patient
can have features of both.

94
00:18:24,192 --> 00:18:36,480
Typical age and diabetes type can guide suspicion but cannot serve as
strict gates. A new presentation may not come with a prior diagnosis
or a familiar medication list.

95
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:49,344
Neither an isolated high glucose nor the absence of fruity breath
supplies the full diagnosis. Laboratory evaluation of ketones, acid-
base balance and osmolality may be needed.

96
00:18:49,344 --> 00:19:02,520
Communicate suspected hyperglycemic crisis and the observed
consequences without inventing a definitive subtype. Timely support
and destination planning do not require perfect classification.

97
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:16,728
The selected modern adult consensus requires ketosis and metabolic
acidosis alongside diabetes or hyperglycemia. It no longer makes the
older Nancy value above eighteen a universal DKA gate.

98
00:19:16,728 --> 00:19:30,360
DKA can occur without marked hyperglycemia, including with SGLT two
medicines, reduced intake or pregnancy. A not-very-high glucose must
not overrule a concerning illness pattern.

99
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:42,552
Most field assessments cannot establish every laboratory criterion.
State the suspicion and what is actually measured rather than
declaring normal glucose means no metabolic emergency.

100
00:19:42,552 --> 00:19:54,216
International hospital insulin and glucose-fluid algorithms are not
new PCP orders. Use the current local pathway, clinical consultation
and urgent definitive assessment.

101
00:19:54,216 --> 00:20:05,568
A fifty-six-year-old with type-two diabetes takes empagliflozin.
During an illness with little intake and repeated vomiting, breathing
becomes deep at twenty-eight per minute.

102
00:20:05,568 --> 00:20:17,400
Glucose is nine point eight. That is below older dramatic DKA values,
but it does not explain away a possible euglycemic ketotic crisis or
another serious cause.

103
00:20:17,400 --> 00:20:30,480
Assess airway, perfusion, glucose reliability and the illness
timeline, and consider other causes of deep breathing such as sepsis.
Do not assume that the drug history alone confirms DKA.

104
00:20:30,480 --> 00:20:43,200
Arrange urgent evaluation, indicated support and a clear alert about
the medicine, reduced intake, vomiting and breathing pattern. Do not
attempt to normalize the number with extra insulin.

105
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:53,280
Support current airway and respiratory needs and prepare for vomiting.
Preserve necessary compensatory ventilation while watching for fatigue
or failure.

106
00:20:53,280 --> 00:21:05,328
Assess actual depletion, perfusion and congestion before an indicated
fluid plan. Cardiac or kidney disease, pregnancy and age can change
the risk and response.

107
00:21:05,328 --> 00:21:18,360
Monitor useful rhythm, glucose and functional trends. Do not
automatically give insulin, bicarbonate or potassium, and do not delay
urgent conveyance for an arbitrary volume target.

108
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:31,440
Seek the precipitant, including infection, missed delivery or another
acute event, and communicate it. Treat access and device problems
compassionately rather than labelling the person noncompliant.

109
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:41,808
Cortisol supports metabolic responses to stress and normal vascular
responsiveness. Deficiency can make an acute illness much harder to
tolerate.

110
00:21:41,808 --> 00:21:52,128
Aldosterone promotes sodium retention and potassium excretion in the
kidney. Primary adrenal failure can reduce both cortisol and
mineralocorticoid function.

111
00:21:52,128 --> 00:22:05,688
Pituitary failure or glucocorticoid suppression can reduce cortisol
while aldosterone remains relatively preserved. Therefore not every
adrenal-crisis patient has high potassium or the same skin findings.

112
00:22:05,688 --> 00:22:19,176
Vomiting, weakness, hypotension, low glucose or confusion in a
compatible history should raise concern. Do not wait for a laboratory
cortisol or a classic pigmentation change to seek help.

113
00:22:19,176 --> 00:22:32,520
Ask about Addison disease, pituitary insufficiency, prior crises and a
prescribed emergency hydrocortisone kit. The patient may carry a
steroid alert card or individualized plan.

114
00:22:32,520 --> 00:22:45,456
The current local guideline uses three weeks or more of glucocorticoid
treatment as a risk-history marker. Actual suppression depends on
exposure and the individual; it is not a perfect bedside test.

115
00:22:45,456 --> 00:22:56,352
Illness, trauma or vomiting can raise need or prevent retention of the
usual replacement. Ask what was taken, kept down or injected before
arrival.

116
00:22:56,352 --> 00:23:09,264
Do not advise abrupt cessation from a chronic steroid history. Excess
exposure can produce Cushing features while the normal stress axis is
suppressed, so appearance alone does not settle reserve.

117
00:23:09,264 --> 00:23:20,664
Current E zero two combines compatible signs such as vomiting,
weakness, hypotension, low glucose or confusion with a qualifying
adrenal or steroid history.

118
00:23:20,664 --> 00:23:31,656
The published pathway also requires an existing hydrocortisone
prescription for adrenal insufficiency. It is not a blanket steroid
order for any undifferentiated shock.

119
00:23:31,656 --> 00:23:47,064
Request the required consultation early and report previous doses. The
guideline addresses treatment even after prior patient administration,
so obtain an explicit plan rather than ignoring or automatically
duplicating that dose.

120
00:23:47,064 --> 00:23:57,000
Continue ABC support and correction of associated low glucose or
hypoperfusion. Do not walk a suspected crisis patient to the
ambulance.

121
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:08,544
The checked adult PCP monograph specifies one hundred milligrams of
hydrocortisone directly over one minute, with prior CliniCall
consultation for acute adrenal crisis.

122
00:24:08,544 --> 00:24:22,224
IV is preferred, but IM is acceptable early if vascular access is
delayed or unobtainable. Do not keep attempting a difficult vein while
the authorized alternative is being unnecessarily delayed.

123
00:24:22,224 --> 00:24:34,920
Use the actual product instructions, trained preparation, indication
and contraindication checks. A one-hundred-milligram vial label does
not by itself tell you the final reconstituted volume.

124
00:24:34,920 --> 00:24:50,448
EMR assistance is limited to collecting the patient own medicine in E
zero two, not preparing or injecting it. Adult PCP authorization does
not establish a pediatric dose or a steroid order for all asthma or
sepsis.

125
00:24:50,448 --> 00:25:01,080
A thirty-eight-year-old with Addison disease and a prescribed rescue
kit has repeated vomiting during an illness. It is unclear how much
usual replacement was retained.

126
00:25:01,080 --> 00:25:12,120
There is weakness and confusion, pressure eighty-two over fifty and
glucose three point two. This needs immediate support and safe
correction of the low glucose.

127
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:23,568
Obtain the actual medicine and prior-dose history, request the
mandatory consultation and use the eligible hydrocortisone pathway
with appropriate training and product checks.

128
00:25:23,568 --> 00:25:35,784
Do not walk the patient or promise immediate normalization after
injection. Reassess perfusion, breathing, glucose and possible
infection while arranging urgent conveyance.

129
00:25:35,784 --> 00:25:48,360
Thyroid hormone influences metabolism, temperature regulation, cardiac
function and responsiveness. The hypothalamus-pituitary-thyroid axis
helps regulate its production.

130
00:25:48,360 --> 00:26:01,536
Low function can cause fatigue, cold intolerance, slowed thinking and
constipation. Excess function can cause heat intolerance, tremor,
rapid pulse and increased metabolic demand.

131
00:26:01,536 --> 00:26:11,376
These chronic patterns do not diagnose an emergency on their own. Ask
about current change, medicine delivery, infection and actual organ
function.

132
00:26:11,376 --> 00:26:26,832
Thyrotoxicosis means excess thyroid-hormone effect and need not always
mean increased gland synthesis. Severe decompensation requires urgent
support and definitive care, not an improvised field thyroid regimen.

133
00:26:26,832 --> 00:26:38,136
Thyroid storm can occur when thyrotoxicosis decompensates during
infection, another stressor or treatment interruption. No single
temperature or pulse establishes it.

134
00:26:38,136 --> 00:26:50,736
Look for the overall severe pattern: marked heat, rapid or irregular
rhythm, gastrointestinal symptoms and altered mental status, sometimes
with heart failure or shock.

135
00:26:50,736 --> 00:27:03,984
Sepsis, toxic exposure and other causes can overlap. Assess glucose,
breathing, perfusion, temperature and the medication/history timeline
while obtaining urgent clinical help.

136
00:27:03,984 --> 00:27:16,104
Provide indicated temperature and ABC support through the actual
pathway. Specialist antithyroid, beta-blocking and other treatments
are not a generic independent PCP bundle.

137
00:27:16,104 --> 00:27:29,472
Severe hypothyroid decompensation is often called myxedema coma,
although the patient need not be fully comatose. Infection, cold,
medicines or another stressor can precipitate it.

138
00:27:29,472 --> 00:27:42,720
Reduced responsiveness, hypoventilation, cold temperature, bradycardia
and hypotension can occur. Check glucose and consider electrolyte
disturbance without diagnosing from one sign.

139
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:54,192
Support effective ventilation and oxygenation, use careful temperature
management and treat confirmed low glucose through the current route.
Escalate urgently for deterioration.

140
00:27:54,192 --> 00:28:07,032
Do not give a textbook dopamine, thyroid-hormone or large dextrose
recipe independently, and do not use age, sex or absent fever to
exclude another serious infection or endocrine cause.

141
00:28:07,032 --> 00:28:32,400
In this original case, a patient with known hypothyroidism has been
ill and is now drowsy, cold and breathing shallowly. Recognize the
breathing and perfusion threats before trying to name the endocrine
diagnosis. Assess airway and effective ventilation, measure glucose
and temperature, and obtain medication and baseline history while your
partner arranges urgent assistance.

142
00:28:32,400 --> 00:29:01,392
Severe hypothyroid decompensation is possible, but infection,
environmental exposure, medicines or more than one problem may
coexist. A lack of fever is not infection clearance. Provide indicated
breathing, glucose, temperature and perfusion support through current
pathways, reassess the response and pre-alert with the observed
findings. Do not independently copy a hormone, vasopressor or fixed
fluid recipe.

143
00:29:01,392 --> 00:29:11,712
ADH, or vasopressin, primarily increases water reabsorption through
its renal effect. This is distinct from aldosterone-mediated sodium
handling.

144
00:29:11,712 --> 00:29:23,808
Deficient ADH effect can cause large dilute urine losses, as in
diabetes insipidus. This is a water-balance disorder, not the same as
diabetes mellitus or glucose in urine.

145
00:29:23,808 --> 00:29:36,216
Excess ADH effect can retain water and contribute to dilutional low
sodium. Neurological symptoms require assessment and proper chemistry
rather than guessing sodium from appearance.

146
00:29:36,216 --> 00:29:50,544
Ask about intake/output, pituitary or brain disease, medicines and the
timeline. Do not automatically give plain water, salt, fluid
restriction or hormone replacement from a suspected label.

147
00:29:50,544 --> 00:30:02,400
Parathyroid hormone helps raise available calcium through kidney and
bone effects and the vitamin-D pathway. Calcium regulation is not
simply a two-hormone on-off switch.

148
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:12,960
Calcitonin is thyroid-derived and has a different role. Keep the
thyroid metabolic-hormone pathway separate from parathyroid and
calcium physiology.

149
00:30:12,960 --> 00:30:25,872
Calcium disturbance can affect neuromuscular and cardiac function,
with possible tingling, spasm, weakness or ECG change. None supplies a
precise blood concentration.

150
00:30:25,872 --> 00:30:38,256
Use the clinical history, actual laboratory information and
appropriate support. A physiology reading does not authorize empiric
calcium for every symptom or a single ECG interval.

151
00:30:38,256 --> 00:30:50,736
Ask what medicine was actually delivered and when, including a pump
interruption, injection issue or vomiting after tablets. A prescribed
plan and delivered treatment are different facts.

152
00:30:50,736 --> 00:31:01,200
Compare that with food, activity, infection symptoms, pregnancy and
other stress. The balance can change even when the patient followed
the usual prescription.

153
00:31:01,200 --> 00:31:12,000
Kidney or liver dysfunction can modify medicine handling, glycogen
availability and fluid risk. Ask about the baseline and verified
recent findings.

154
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:25,992
Explore practical access barriers without blame and use the patient
preferred communication support. Do not change an insulin pump program
or long-term regimen without actual authority and an agreed clinical
plan.

155
00:31:25,992 --> 00:31:37,632
After correction of low glucose, ask whether the actual problem
resolved. A patient can have a normal value and still have a stroke,
infection, injury or toxic effect.

156
00:31:37,632 --> 00:31:51,480
In a suspected high-glucose crisis, follow breathing, perfusion and
rhythm with the illness course. A lower glucose alone does not prove
resolution of ketosis, acidosis or hyperosmolality.

157
00:31:51,480 --> 00:32:03,408
In adrenal crisis, accurate eligibility, dose history and early
consultation matter alongside ABC support. Do not assume that a
hormone injection instantly reverses shock.

158
00:32:03,408 --> 00:32:14,880
If the response does not fit, reconsider delivery, timing, recurrence
and another cause. Explain that revision clearly to the team and
receiving clinicians.

159
00:32:14,880 --> 00:32:24,768
Begin with the current patient, not only a glucose number. State
awareness, breathing, circulation and the reason the case needs urgent
evaluation.

160
00:32:24,768 --> 00:32:37,512
Give measured glucose with units and times, relevant medicines or
established hormone disease and the illness timeline. Preserve last
known well when neurological disease remains possible.

161
00:32:37,512 --> 00:32:50,304
Report the actual product, amount, concentration, route and time,
including earlier rescue doses and the required consultation. Then
describe both the number and functional response.

162
00:32:50,304 --> 00:33:02,904
Mention recurrence, persistent focal findings, possible euglycemic
crisis or unresolved shock. Accurate uncertainty helps the receiving
team prepare the right investigation and care.

163
00:33:02,904 --> 00:33:13,776
For low glucose, explain the route from command following, swallowing
and IV availability, then calculate the actual product volume and
predict the response.

164
00:33:13,776 --> 00:33:25,800
For suspected DKA or HHS, explain two mechanisms and why one glucose
does not describe the complete problem. Include ventilation and
potassium risk.

165
00:33:25,800 --> 00:33:39,000
For adrenal crisis, state the compatible illness, qualifying history,
prescription and mandatory consultation. Include the adult regimen and
what you do if IV access is delayed.

166
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:49,728
Finish each answer with an alternative diagnosis and a reassessment
that could change your plan. Naming a hormone or a medicine is only
the beginning of the explanation.

167
00:33:49,728 --> 00:33:59,472
You should now explain the major endocrine feedback and glucose-
regulation roles without using age, body size or an insulin label as a
diagnostic shortcut.

168
00:33:59,472 --> 00:34:09,216
You should choose an airway-safe low-glucose route, calculate D ten
correctly, distinguish the current nasal glucagon product and
investigate recurrence.

169
00:34:09,216 --> 00:34:20,880
You should connect DKA and HHS mechanisms to breathing, fluid and
potassium risk, and recognize adrenal or thyroid decompensation with
actual local treatment boundaries.

170
00:34:20,880 --> 00:34:30,792
Complete the bilingual reading and original practice, then use the
integrated school cases to rehearse a changing-patient assessment and
accurate handover.
